# tokio-tar at the fixed revision 22b3f88 (CVE-2025-62518, CWE-436/CWE-20)
# sizes every entry's data read from the ustar header's octal size field alone.
# POSIX gives a pax extended header's size record plain precedence over that
# field -- the record exists to carry values the fixed octal field cannot, and
# its size value is the size of the following entry's data. The walker
# captures the raw extended-header bytes for each entry and hands them to the
# entry for path resolution, but the function that frames the data read never
# receives them: it reads Header::entry_size and bounds the reader with take,
# and no extended-header consult appears in it at all. The regression test the
# fix ships builds an archive whose blob entry declares a larger extended size
# than its header size, and the lenient reader ends the entry early, so the
# next entry's header is read from inside the blob's data -- the entry walk
# itself is fed attacker-chosen bytes.
#
# src/archive.rs poll_next_raw at the vulnerable revision bf266ff is the whole shape:
#
#     let file_pos = *next;
#     let size = header.entry_size()?;
#
#     let mut data = VecDeque::with_capacity(1);
#     data.push_back(EntryIo::Data(archive.clone().take(size)));
#
# The fixed revision threads the captured pax bytes into the framing function and applies
# the size record before the take:
#
#     let mut size = header.entry_size()?;
#     if let Some(pax) = pax_extensions_data.map(pax_extensions) {
#         for extension in pax {
#             ...
#             match key {
#                 "size" => { size = size_str.parse::<u64>()?; }
#                 ...
#
# This is the second measured shape of the extended-header precedence family:
# the conditional half (the record consulted but applied only when the base
# field is zero) is bound separately on its own atoms, and the two stay
# separate because their evidence names different things -- a zero-comparison
# this shape does not carry, and a consult this shape does not contain.
#
# What fires at the vulnerable revision: nothing on the defect. `scan --cache off` over
# the vulnerable checkout reports 76 findings -- 39 MEM-006, 33 DOS-042, two
# MEM-005, a PATH-083 and a PATH-001 -- including nine NullableDeref rows on
# the header unwraps inside poll_next_raw itself, and none on the size
# derivation that is the bug. With this rule both revisions report 77 and the
# diff between them is exactly two rows: this one at src/archive.rs:498 on
# the parent only, and a DOS-042 row the 23 lines of regression test the fixing commit
# commit itself adds on the fixed side only. Three runs per side, identical
# each time. The positive atoms name the tar family's own surface -- entry_size
# appears in no other rust tree among the campaign's clones -- so the
# reachable set outside this crate is empty.
#
# Vocabulary checked for reuse before minting. code.ConditionalExtendedHeaderOverride
# is the same interpretation conflict over the same record, but its condition
# names a zero-comparison on the base field that this shape does not carry --
# here the override is absent, not conditional -- so reusing the label would
# state a condition the flagged code does not satisfy. code.AmbiguousMessageBodyFraming
# is the HTTP/1 Transfer-Encoding pair, code.AmbiguousArchiveManifestSelection
# is about which entry a manifest lookup selects, and the ArchiveSymlinkFilterBypass
# family keys on entry names and paths. None covers "the extended-header
# records are never consulted where the format gives them precedence", so
# code.AbsentExtendedHeaderOverride is minted for that class.
#
# It is bound in Rust, where the tar crate family publishes both halves:
# Header::entry_size reads the base header's size field, and the reader's
// take bounds the bytes handed to the entry. The consult is recognised on
# the published pax_extensions surface in any spelling this family uses --
# a bare function value passed to map, a direct call, or the
# pax_extensions_value lookup helper -- so the fixed revision and the sibling crate's
# unconditional form both clear it.
#
# Deliberately uncovered, and why: a parser consulting the records through
# an accessor this family does not name. The negation keys on the published
# surface, so a differently-named consult reads as absent and would be
# reported; there is no second measured instance to pin more spellings on.

test "tokio-tar: entry data sized from the base header alone while no pax consult appears in the framing function"
  lang rust
  file src/archive.rs
  expect VYQL-VAL-035
  expect_review code.AbsentExtendedHeaderOverride
  code
  ```
  use std::io;

  struct Header;

  impl Header {
      fn new_old() -> Header {
          Header
      }

      fn entry_size(&self) -> io::Result<u64> {
          Ok(0)
      }
  }

  struct Archive;

  impl Clone for Archive {
      fn clone(&self) -> Archive {
          Archive
      }
  }

  impl Archive {
      fn take(&self, _size: u64) -> Archive {
          Archive
      }
  }

  struct EntryIo;

  impl EntryIo {
      fn data(_archive: Archive) -> EntryIo {
          EntryIo
      }
  }

  fn poll_next_raw(next: u64) -> io::Result<u64> {
      let header = Header::new_old();
      let file_pos = next;
      let size = header.entry_size()?;

      let mut data = VecDeque::with_capacity(1);
      data.push_back(EntryIo::data(Archive.clone().take(size)));
      let _ = data;
      let _ = file_pos;
      Ok(size)
  }
  ```

test "tokio-tar: a pax consult in a sibling function does not clear the flag on the framing function"
  lang rust
  file src/archive.rs
  expect VYQL-VAL-035
  expect_review code.AbsentExtendedHeaderOverride
  code
  ```
  use std::io;

  struct Header;

  impl Header {
      fn new_old() -> Header {
          Header
      }

      fn entry_size(&self) -> io::Result<u64> {
          Ok(0)
      }
  }

  struct Archive;

  impl Clone for Archive {
      fn clone(&self) -> Archive {
          Archive
      }
  }

  impl Archive {
      fn take(&self, _size: u64) -> Archive {
          Archive
      }
  }

  struct EntryIo;

  impl EntryIo {
      fn data(_archive: Archive) -> EntryIo {
          EntryIo
      }
  }

  fn poll_next_raw() -> io::Result<u64> {
      let header = Header::new_old();
